         <!--Fl(avio) Cesario et Noni/o Attio cons(ulibu)s depo/sita Alexandra III / idus novembres / ann(os) V mensum / VIII dies V quiens/quit in pace --><div type="edition" xml:lang="la"><head>Text</head><ab><lb n="1"/><expan><abbr>Fl</abbr><ex>avio</ex></expan> Cesario et Noni<lb break="no" n="2"/>o Attio cons<expan><ex>ulibu</ex><abbr>s</abbr></expan> depo<lb break="no" n="3"/>sita Alexandra III<lb n="4"/>idus novembres<lb n="5"/><expan><abbr>ann</abbr><ex>os</ex></expan> V mensum<lb n="6"/>VIII dies V quiens<lb break="no" n="7"/>quit in pace </ab></div>
         <div type="apparatus">
            <p>Silvagni in ICVR ad loc. cit. "Ut de Rossi animadvertit, veteris tituli novicium hoc est exemplum, in quo vitio recentis quadratario in v.2 ATTIO pro ATTICO scriptum". Contra Billanovich 1967: "una lapida catalogata da un giudice sottile (de Rossi) tra le copie invece risulterà autentica". Billanovich in support of her theory indicates the frequency of stonecutters' mistakes even in ancient inscriptions; paleographic and test layout features,the  guidelines, and the absence in the manuscript and lapidary tradition of an urban original of which it is a copy.</p>
         </div>
      </body>
